# ops/awcms-one-backup.crontab — issue #213.
#
# THIS FILE IS A MANIFEST, NOT A LIVE CONFIGURATION — the same convention as
# apps/cms/deploy/cron/awcms.crontab and apps/cms/ops/awcms-jobs.crontab
# (issue #150's own `ops/run-job-compose.sh` pattern). Nothing in this repo
# reads it, no deploy applies it, and merging it schedules nothing. An
# operator installs it on the HOST that runs `compose.production.yaml` (not
# inside any container):
#
#   sudo cp ops/awcms-one-backup.crontab /etc/cron.d/awcms-one-backup   # then edit the vars
#   sudo chown root:root /etc/cron.d/awcms-one-backup
#   sudo chmod 0644 /etc/cron.d/awcms-one-backup
#
# (/etc/cron.d entries need a USER field between the schedule and the
# command — see the comment at the bottom of this file. As written below,
# each line is a USER crontab line, `crontab -u <deploy-user>
# ops/awcms-one-backup.crontab`, which needs no user field.)
#
# ## Why this file exists, separate from apps/cms/deploy/cron/awcms.crontab
#
# That file documents the Coolify one-shot-container invocation pattern
# (`docker run --network container:<db-container> ...`) — correct for a
# Coolify-managed database with no published port and no compose project of
# its own. This repository's OWN production topology
# (`compose.production.yaml`, ADR-0019) is different: `postgres` is a named
# compose service reachable by DNS on the compose project's own network, and
# `backup`/`restore-drill`/`offsite-copy` are compose SERVICES (profile-
# gated, so a plain `docker compose up` never starts them) built from
# `docker/backup/Dockerfile` — a thin image adding only `age`/`rsync`/
# `openssh-client` on top of the SAME `postgres:18.4` the `postgres` service
# runs. `ops/run-backup-compose.sh` is the one stable command this crontab
# calls for all three; see docs/deployment.md's "Backup assurance" section
# for the full runbook (key generation, secrets layout, what each task does).
#
# ## Database identity
#
# `backup` and `restore-drill` both connect as the SAME migration-owner role
# `migrate` uses (`SETUP_DATABASE_URL` in `apps/cms/.env` /
# `compose.production.yaml`'s own root `.env`) — never `awcms_app` (`cms`)
# or `awcms_worker` (`jobs`). See compose.production.yaml's own `backup`/
# `restore-drill` service comments for why (pg_dump needs to read every
# table; the drill's scratch database needs CREATEDB).
#
# ## Safety — restore-drill can NEVER touch production from this file
#
# `restore-drill` below always runs `ops/run-backup-compose.sh restore-drill`
# — no flag, argument, or environment variable this crontab line could carry
# changes that. `compose.production.yaml`'s `restore-drill` service has a
# hard-coded `command: ["bash", "/scripts/restore-drill.sh"]`, and
# `restore-drill.sh` itself has no `--target` code path at all (see its own
# header in apps/cms/deploy/backup/). A real disaster-recovery restore is
# deliberately NOT scheduled anywhere — it is the manual, confirmed
# `docker run ... restore-postgres.sh <dump> --target=<db> --yes` runbook
# step in docs/deployment.md's "Production runbook".

SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
CRON_TZ=UTC

# EDIT THIS ONE LINE, then the job lines need no further changes. Must be an
# absolute path to the checkout that has `compose.production.yaml` and
# `ops/run-backup-compose.sh` at the paths shown (the same checkout the
# `cms`/`jobs`/`storefront` images are built from).
AWCMS_ONE_REPO=/opt/awcms-one
AWCMS_ONE_BACKUP_LOG=/var/log/awcms-one

# ===========================================================================
# Daily — staggered so the nightly work does not land in one heap, and so a
# failed backup is visible before the off-site copy would otherwise run
# against nothing new.
# ===========================================================================

# Backup, encrypted (when BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENTS_FILE/BACKUP_HMAC_KEY_FILE
# secrets are configured — see docs/deployment.md) or plain otherwise.
0 2 * * * cd $AWCMS_ONE_REPO && ./ops/run-backup-compose.sh backup >> $AWCMS_ONE_BACKUP_LOG/backup.log 2>&1

# Off-site copy, right after the backup completes. Requires OFFSITE_SSH_TARGET
# (and OFFSITE_SSH_KEY_FILE) configured — see docs/deployment.md. Never
# deletes the local copy regardless of transfer outcome, and never reports a
# failed transfer as a success (offsite-copy.sh's own header).
15 2 * * * cd $AWCMS_ONE_REPO && ./ops/run-backup-compose.sh offsite-copy >> $AWCMS_ONE_BACKUP_LOG/offsite-copy.log 2>&1

# ===========================================================================
# Weekly — a backup nobody restores is not a backup.
# ===========================================================================

# Unattended, non-destructive restore drill: selects the newest eligible
# backup, verifies + decrypts it, restores into a scratch database, checks
# it, drops it, and appends evidence to
# ${BACKUP_DIR}/restore-drill-evidence.jsonl (inside the named
# `awcms-one-production-backups` volume). Never touches a live database —
# see the safety note above. Runs on Sunday, an hour after that night's
# backup.
0 3 * * 0 cd $AWCMS_ONE_REPO && ./ops/run-backup-compose.sh restore-drill >> $AWCMS_ONE_BACKUP_LOG/restore-drill.log 2>&1

# ===========================================================================
# Installing into /etc/cron.d instead of a user crontab
# ===========================================================================
#
# /etc/cron.d entries take a USER field between the schedule and the command:
#
#   0 2 * * * awcms-one cd $AWCMS_ONE_REPO && ./ops/run-backup-compose.sh backup >> ...
#             ^^^^^^^^^
#
# That user must be able to run `docker compose` against this project (in the
# `docker` group, which is root-equivalent on most hosts — the same
# trade-off apps/cms/deploy/cron/awcms.crontab's own installing section
# documents). Add the field to every line if you take that route; leave the
# file as-is for `crontab -u <deploy-user> ops/awcms-one-backup.crontab`.
#
# `mkdir -p /var/log/awcms-one` and a logrotate rule for
# /var/log/awcms-one/*.log before installing — these three log files append
# forever otherwise.
#
# A user crontab needs a trailing newline at end of file or the last entry is
# silently dropped by some cron implementations. There is one below.
